WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.438
So I’m working on a Horror History script

00:00:01.438 --> 00:00:03.683
about a villain who has sparked a lot of debate among fans

00:00:03.683 --> 00:00:07.605
with many people taking&nbsp;this villain’s side and saying that this person was actually justified.

00:00:07.605 --> 00:00:10.595
I’m not ready&nbsp;to say who this person is yet, but it got me thinking...

00:00:10.595 --> 00:00:13.200
Are there any villains who were&nbsp;actually doing the right thing?

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.844
So I put up a post asking my audience for input, challenging&nbsp;you guys to name a villain who was in the right.

00:00:17.844 --> 00:00:21.035
And I also spent the day thinking&nbsp;about it, and basically decided…

00:00:21.035 --> 00:00:24.667
uh no, if they were in the right then they&nbsp;wouldn’t be considered the villain. Right?

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:26.168
But after mulling this over a little&nbsp;further,

00:00:26.168 --> 00:00:28.934
I do think there may be a couple villains who qualify.

00:00:28.934 --> 00:00:31.464
To find out&nbsp;who, stick around to the end of this video.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:36.751
♪ Metal Music ♪

00:00:37.792 --> 00:00:40.094
So then I started thinking about&nbsp;the villains that came to mind,

00:00:40.094 --> 00:00:43.621
and looking at some of the answers you guys&nbsp;provided, I started seeing a few trends.

00:00:43.621 --> 00:00:47.070
There were a few different categories that all&nbsp;of these villains seemed to fall into.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.703
But let's lay down the technical definitions&nbsp;before continuing.

00:00:49.703 --> 00:00:53.260
So a villain is a character whose evil actions or motives are important to&nbsp;the plot.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.437
The protagonist is just the leading character in a work of fiction, and the&nbsp;antagonist opposes that leading character.

00:00:58.437 --> 00:01:02.101
Usually the antagonist is the villain, but in&nbsp;some cases, the protagonist is the villain.

00:01:02.101 --> 00:01:07.401
I went into this video thinking that there&nbsp;wouldn’t be any examples of villains who I found to have actions that were just…

00:01:07.401 --> 00:01:09.330
but&nbsp;some of these characters surprised me.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.160
Just&nbsp;as you were probably surprised that I finally&nbsp;uploaded something other than Horror History.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.120
Also, seeing as how this *isn’t*&nbsp;an episode of Horror History,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:21.261
I won’t go too in depth about any of the&nbsp;characters that I talk about here, but there&nbsp;may be spoilers for the following stories.&nbsp;

00:01:21.261 --> 00:01:23.440
Primarily, Gone Girl and Cabin in the Woods.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.717
So with that out of the way let's&nbsp;jump into the first category.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.444
♪ Hard Rock Music ♪

00:01:29.444 --> 00:01:33.656
In my post, there was one character&nbsp;who was the overwhelming favorite in&nbsp;&nbsp;the replies

00:01:33.656 --> 00:01:36.342
and that was Carrie&nbsp;White from Stephen King’s Carrie.

00:01:36.342 --> 00:01:40.443
–[Carrie] It was bad Mama. They laughed at me.

00:01:40.443 --> 00:01:44.393
To which I have to say… are you kidding me? 
Carrie&nbsp;White was not in the right.

00:01:44.393 --> 00:01:47.405
Now I did kind of bait&nbsp;&nbsp;people by putting her in the image, but still!

00:01:47.405 --> 00:01:49.834
The&nbsp;fact that so many people chose her is kind of concerning!

00:01:49.834 --> 00:01:53.280
Carrie is a teenage girl who is abused at&nbsp;home by her overly religious mother while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.708
being bullied and socially outcast at school by&nbsp;her cruel classmates.&nbsp;

00:01:56.708 --> 00:02:00.960
When Carrie hits puberty,&nbsp;it coincides with an awakening of her&nbsp;telekinetic powers, so when one of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.240
classmates orchestrates an extremely cruel prank&nbsp;by dumping pig’s blood on her at the Senior Prom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.200
Carrie snaps, kills her classmates and then&nbsp;rampages into the the town and massacres it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.400
starting a huge fire and telekinetically twisting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.160
the fire hydrant lug nuts so tight that&nbsp;the fire department could not access the water.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:20.000
Now I’m not saying that Carrie is pure evil, and&nbsp;the people who bullied and abused her are perfect&nbsp;angels.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.351
They can both be bad, and they are. But&nbsp;two wrongs do not make a right. 

00:02:23.351 --> 00:02:27.019
If someone does&nbsp;something to you, and you do it back to them,&nbsp; you’re now on the same level.

00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:31.640
But Carrie is even worse than that because she not only got revenge&nbsp;on her bullies, but also killed innocent people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.690
Attacking in self-defense is OK, but that’s&nbsp;not what’s going on here.&nbsp;

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:39.240
If we were to take&nbsp;telekinetic abilities out of the equation for a&nbsp;moment, this is essentially a school shooting.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:44.000
Nobody is out there saying that the Columbine&nbsp;shooters did the right thing by shooting up&nbsp;their school because they were bullied. 

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.920
So&nbsp;why are people saying that about Carrie?

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.734
I’m guessing it’s because, in the movie, the story&nbsp;is told from Carrie’s point of view. &nbsp;

00:02:49.734 --> 00:02:53.440
We see the&nbsp;bullying, and I think it’s normal to feel bad for&nbsp;her. I’m guessing a lot of the people who said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.120
Carrie have been bullied themselves at some point,&nbsp;and they are able to share in her frustration and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.867
want to protect her because they relate to her&nbsp;in some small way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.867 --> 00:03:05.375
And I think there’s just a little bit of disconnect here. I can understand&nbsp;thinking the antagonists deserved some kind of&nbsp;punishment

00:03:05.375 --> 00:03:08.880
and maybe some people got that mixed&nbsp;up with saying that Carrie was in the right.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.926
There were several answers that I put under this&nbsp;same category of “they deserved it tho”. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.926 --> 00:03:17.080
Amazing Amy Dunne from Gone Girl staged her disappearance&nbsp;to make it look like her husband had killed her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:21.560
to get back at him for his infidelity, but she&nbsp;also murders an innocent guy who took her in.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.152
Another example is Frankenstein’s monster from&nbsp;Mary Shelley’s Frankenstein.&nbsp;

00:03:25.152 --> 00:03:29.890
The monster was&nbsp;created by a scientist named Victor Frankenstein,&nbsp;obsessed with the idea of creating life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:34.320
Maybe just get a GF, my guy. Repulsed by his&nbsp;creation, Victor abandons the monster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.867
who then tries to connect with other humans,&nbsp;who are afraid of him, causing him to become&nbsp;bitter and violent. 

00:03:38.867 --> 00:03:42.360
It’s OK to feel bad for the&nbsp;monster, who didn’t ask to be brought into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.000
world where he would be rejected, but I&nbsp;can’t fully justify hurting other people.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.880
I would be more willing to accept&nbsp;Frankenstein’s Monster as a villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.460
that was in the right than Carrie White,&nbsp;because you could make the argument that&nbsp;&nbsp;the monster didn’t know better.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.480
But then&nbsp;you start getting into the territory of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.880
“maybe the monster is not the villain.” Maybe&nbsp;*Victor Frankenstein* is the villain, the man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.280
who tried to play God by creating life, but failed&nbsp;to take responsibility for what he had created.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:07.976
You could argue that Victor Frankenstein was a&nbsp;villain who was in the right though.

00:04:07.976 --> 00:04:13.834
After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;he was just trying to advance humanity through&nbsp;science. Sure he made mistakes, but it was in the&nbsp;&nbsp;pursuit of knowledge.

00:04:13.834 --> 00:04:18.034
If we consider him to be the&nbsp;villain, he would fall into a different category.

00:04:18.034 --> 00:04:20.760
♪ Opera Music ♪

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.000
Elijah Price was a boy born with a condition&nbsp;called osteogenesis imperfecta.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.333
His bones were extremely fragile, so he spent most&nbsp;of his life broken and in wheelchairs.

00:04:29.333 --> 00:04:31.200
–[Elijah] They called me Mr. Glass.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.499
While he was physically very fragile,&nbsp;he was extremely gifted from a mental&nbsp;standpoint.

00:04:35.499 --> 00:04:38.400
He theorized that if someone&nbsp;like him exists on one end of the spectrum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.586
there must be a hero who is incredibly&nbsp;durable on the other end. 

00:04:41.586 --> 00:04:45.480
He carries out&nbsp;a series of terrorist attacks in search of&nbsp;this person, eventually finding a man named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.200
David Dunne who is the only survivor&nbsp;of a trainwreck caused by Mr. Glass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.840
He spends the rest of the story encouraging&nbsp;David to take on the role of a superhero.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.520
When one of you guys suggested him&nbsp;as a villain who was in the right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.080
it was because he made&nbsp;sacrifices with a noble goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.240
and discovered an actual superhero&nbsp;who would go on to protect the city.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.720
The villains in this category mostly&nbsp;all fall under the same moral dilemma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.385
It’s basically a more complicated version of&nbsp;the infamous trolley problem. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.385 --> 00:05:12.240
If five people are tied to one side of the track,&nbsp;and one person is tied to the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.040
is it OK to pull the lever and kill the&nbsp;one person in order to save the five?

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:21.980
According to tokresource.org, about 90% of&nbsp;people would pull the lever and kill the one&nbsp;person to save the five. 

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:25.264
So that settles&nbsp;it, Mr. Glass was in the right… right?

00:05:25.264 --> 00:05:27.160
– ACKCHYUALLY, allow me to correct you.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.975
The trolley problem only has two options, pull&nbsp;the lever or don’t. 

00:05:30.975 --> 00:05:37.202
Well, I always like to say&nbsp;you could pull during the merge point, and&nbsp;derail the trolley. Depends if anyone’s on&nbsp;the trolley and how fast it’s going.

00:05:37.202 --> 00:05:39.880
But my&nbsp;point is, if I were in Mr. Glass’s position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:45.835
and I believed there was a superhero out there who&nbsp;needed to be discovered, I wouldn’t settle for a&nbsp;plan that involved killing other people.

00:05:45.835 --> 00:05:48.000
He could&nbsp;have just gotten involved in contacting survivors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.880
of the many accidents that *already* happen,&nbsp;or held some kind of amateur sports tournament.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:57.285
There are definitely less evil ways he could have&nbsp;gone about it. But he essentially wanted to be the&nbsp;villain,

00:05:57.285 --> 00:06:00.960
because in his comic books, the hero&nbsp;and villain are opposites in almost every way.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.111
Mr. Glass wasn’t in the right, but I think&nbsp;we are getting closer.

00:06:04.111 --> 00:06:10.193
Our next villain also&nbsp;took up the diabolical podium out of what he saw&nbsp;as necessity. But the stakes were much higher.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:14.418
(IMPACT)

00:06:15.554 --> 00:06:19.706
♪ Upbeat Music ♪

00:06:19.706 --> 00:06:24.000
Lelouch Lamperouge from Code Geass wanted to&nbsp;overthrow the oppressive Britannian Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.920
but makes many morally questionable sacrifices&nbsp;to do it, including controlling others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.340
committing acts of terrorism,&nbsp;and betraying his friends.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:33.820
–[Lelouch] What do you mean there’s only soup?
–[Suzaku] It means there’s only soup.&nbsp;

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:37.480
– [Lelouch] Well then get out of the soup aisle!
– [Suzaku] Alright, you don’t have to shout at me!

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:39.320
I love how there are gonna be&nbsp;people who watch this video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:42.620
who have never seen Code Geass and they&nbsp;now think those are their actual voices.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.640
– [Lelouch] WHAT DO YOU MEAN YOU’RE AT SOUP?

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:47.768
A couple of you brought up Joseph Seed&nbsp;from Far Cry 5.

00:06:47.768 --> 00:06:51.840
Joseph was essentially&nbsp;a cult leader who believes he was&nbsp;chosen by God to gather followers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:55.366
and lead them to “Eden’s Gate” to protect&nbsp;them from the incoming apocalypse. &nbsp;

00:06:55.366 --> 00:06:58.524
However,&nbsp;he’s willing to use violence and torture to&nbsp;advance his goals.

00:06:58.524 --> 00:07:03.560
At one point he pinches&nbsp;the tube on his own daughter’s life support,&nbsp;to kill her and prove his devotion to God.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:07.240
Under normal circumstances, he’d be seen as&nbsp;your run of the mill religious extremist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.926
no better than some of the worst&nbsp;terrorists of our world.

00:07:09.926 --> 00:07:15.040
However,&nbsp;in the end, the apocalypse actually happens&nbsp;just as he predicted, down to the very moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:18.320
making it seem as if Joseph really&nbsp;was receiving the word of God.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.351
Given that information, it’s hard to say that&nbsp;he wasn’t in the right. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.351 --> 00:07:24.506
Any of the evil actions that he took before this point don’t really&nbsp;matter.

00:07:24.506 --> 00:07:29.920
Everyone who wasn’t a follower of&nbsp;his would be wiped out anyways, so the guy&nbsp;did what he had to do to save humanity.&nbsp;

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:33.960
The story has an interesting message, because the&nbsp;religious extremists of our world probably do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:37.480
believe that they are serving God, and do&nbsp;see their evil actions as heroic.&nbsp;

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:40.849
I think&nbsp;Joseph Seed forces the audience to see the&nbsp;world through their eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.849 --> 00:07:44.800
How do you deal&nbsp;with these people when they see themselves&nbsp;as divine? 
It’s an interesting quandary.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.000
Disclaimer, although I do think Joseph is one&nbsp;of the best examples of a righteous villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.400
I am not condoning any kind of religious violence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:54.520
I don’t want to see any news articles&nbsp;about the CZsWorld pipeline, OK?

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:58.351
The world of superheroes offers a couple other&nbsp;examples of villains in this category.&nbsp;

00:07:58.351 --> 00:08:02.332
Poison&nbsp;Ivy from Batman engages in acts of terror in&nbsp;defense of the environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.332 --> 00:08:07.080
Thanos partook in what amounts to a mass genocide by snapping&nbsp;away half of all life in the universe, which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:11.760
sees as a virtuous act to curb overpopulation&nbsp;and reduce strain on the world’s resources.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:15.583
I hope I got that right. I’m not actually in the&nbsp;know when it comes to the world of Marvel,&nbsp;

00:08:15.583 --> 00:08:21.517
but I&nbsp;do know a little bit about Thanos just by being&nbsp;so into internet culture. 
I mean -- the Thanos&nbsp;car? f*cking hilarious.

00:08:21.517 --> 00:08:25.240
Ant Man’s plan to defeat&nbsp;Thanos? One of the best things I’ve ever seen.

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:29.610
Now when I was making my list, there were&nbsp;some characters that I ended up moving into&nbsp;a sub-category. 

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:32.680
They weren’t just sacrificing&nbsp;justice in the name of something they believe in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:37.000
they were taking justice into their&nbsp;own hands. These are the vigilantes.

00:08:43.682 --> 00:08:46.720
In the series known as Death Note,&nbsp;Light Yagami was a high school student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:50.667
who happened upon a notebook that had&nbsp;the power to kill anyone whose name&nbsp;is written into it.

00:08:50.667 --> 00:08:52.960
After testing it&nbsp;on a criminal who had taken hostages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:57.360
he’s horrified by its power and resolves to&nbsp;get rid of it… but then he has another idea.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:02.024
Light is going to use the notebook to&nbsp;take out ALL of the world’s criminals&nbsp;so that only the good remain. 

00:09:02.024 --> 00:09:05.667
He wants to&nbsp;create a new world, a utopia with no bad&nbsp;people or crime.

00:09:05.667 --> 00:09:09.504
This approach is popular,&nbsp;and Light, or as he becomes known, “Kira”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.504 --> 00:09:12.800
amasses followers all over the world&nbsp;who realizes someone is dishing out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:17.240
punishment to criminals and supports his&nbsp;endeavor. Others see him as just as bad or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:21.240
worse than those he is killing, and seek to&nbsp;
hunt down and arrest this new serial killer.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:25.280
The debate in the series has extended to real&nbsp;life, 
with fans arguing over whether Light was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:29.203
a good guy or a bad guy nonstop in the 20&nbsp;
years since the series came out. 

00:09:29.203 --> 00:09:32.720
As a fan,&nbsp;&nbsp;I’ve spent a lot of time thinking about&nbsp;this series, so it’s easy for me to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:37.947
that while Light has good intentions,&nbsp;
it is evil for any one man to be judge,&nbsp;jury and executioner. 

00:09:37.947 --> 00:09:40.570
The legal system has&nbsp;split these roles up for a reason.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:46.267
Having one person handle all three is too much power,&nbsp;and that leads to corruption, which is exactly&nbsp;what happens to Light.

00:09:46.267 --> 00:09:49.640
One person’s judgment can&nbsp;never be unbiased, and may not always be fair.

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:54.285
Vigilante Justice stories are something of a&nbsp;genre of their own. Billy from Silent Night, Deadly Night

00:09:54.285 --> 00:09:56.725
similarly targeted those who&nbsp;were naughty before Christmas.

00:09:56.725 --> 00:10:00.960
Clyde Shelton&nbsp;from Law Abiding Citizen took revenge on&nbsp;corrupt police officers and criminals who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:04.099
were involved in the mis-sentencing of the man&nbsp;who killed his wife and daughter.&nbsp;

00:10:04.099 --> 00:10:09.899
Similarly,&nbsp;Dexter Morgan from the TV series Dexter, was&nbsp;a forensics specialist by day, and serial&nbsp;killer by night,

00:10:09.899 --> 00:10:12.960
only going after heinous&nbsp;criminals who had escaped the legal system.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.440
Then there’s John Kramer, or as&nbsp;he’s more commonly known, Jigsaw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:20.840
from the Saw franchise, who subjected his targets&nbsp;
to agonizing torture devices which he believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:25.240
would reinvigorate those who had lost their&nbsp;
appreciation for life… assuming they survived.

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:26.720
Don’t worry, the video’s not over.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:30.680
Perhaps the best example in the wider “Made&nbsp;Sacrifices For The Greater Good” category would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:34.960
be The Director from The Cabin in the Woods,&nbsp;the head of a secret organization whose goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:39.000
is to sacrifice five human archetypes to&nbsp;a race of ancient Gods living underground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:43.120
in order to entertain them and satisfy a&nbsp;ritual. 
If the ritual fails, the Ancient&nbsp;Ones

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:46.920
will rise up and destroy the earth, so&nbsp;the organization puts a lot of resources into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:50.536
creating monsters capable of terrorizing&nbsp;and killing the young subjects.

00:10:50.536 --> 00:10:53.840
It’s hard&nbsp;&nbsp;to argue against what the Director and the&nbsp;rest of the organization are doing here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:56.840
because they’re literally saving the&nbsp;world the only way they know how.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:11:00.234
However, they may also fall&nbsp;more into another category.

00:11:00.351 --> 00:11:02.622
♪ Ragtime Music ♪

00:11:02.622 --> 00:11:07.400
One of the top answers to my query outside of&nbsp;
Carrie White… which… occupied the top three spots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:11.411
was Parker Crane -- also known as&nbsp;
The Bride in Black from Insidious.

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:17.760
As a boy, Parker was abused by his&nbsp;mother, Michelle, who forced him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:21.360
dress up like a girl because she didn’t like&nbsp;him going by the name his father gave him.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:28.412
–[Michelle] THAT IS NOT YOUR NAME! PARKER&nbsp;is not your name!&nbsp;

00:11:28.412 --> 00:11:36.240
That is the&nbsp;name your father gave you! 
Your name… is Marilyn.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:40.400
What’s the clinical name for this type of&nbsp;behavior? Oh yeah. She kinda cray-cray!

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:44.740
In life, she forced Parker to do horrible things,&nbsp;basically molding him into a serial killer.

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:48.520
–[Parker] If she sees you, she’ll make me kill you.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:53.160
Even after she died, she continued to manipulate&nbsp;him from the afterlife realm known as The Further.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:57.984
After she’s eradicated from The Further, 
Parker&nbsp;basically just fades away. Parker did do evil&nbsp;things,

00:11:57.984 --> 00:12:01.800
but he was controlled from such a young&nbsp;
age, and the grip was kept on him for so long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:05.720
that it almost seems like it was unavoidable. 
You&nbsp;could make the argument that Michelle is the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:09.280
