00:13:41.592 --> 00:13:42.810
♪ Ragtime Music ♪

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:47.120
Amelia, the mother in the Babadook is entranced&nbsp;to kill her dog and nearly her son when she’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:50.560
possessed by this monster from her child’s&nbsp;bedtime story. This one is going to open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:54.800
up an interesting can of worms… or perhaps I&nbsp;should say “bowl of worms” because the monster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:58.320
is a metaphor for her grief and depression,&nbsp;which she’s unable to get under control.

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:03.007
So on one hand, she can’t use the puppet&nbsp;excuse, all of her actions are actually&nbsp;coming from her.

00:14:03.007 --> 00:14:05.384
She didn’t get a handle&nbsp;on her grief, so it’s her fault. &nbsp;

00:14:05.384 --> 00:14:08.904
However, you could make the argument that she is&nbsp;just a victim of the circumstances.

00:14:08.904 --> 00:14:13.218
Yes, she’s mentally ill, but it largely&nbsp;stems from her husband’s unexpected&nbsp;death.

00:14:13.218 --> 00:14:16.200
She didn’t ask for any of it, is&nbsp;it really her fault that she’s struggling?

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:19.000
Personally, I think you have to hold her&nbsp;accountable for what she did.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.120
If you give&nbsp;a pass to everyone who does something&nbsp;bad and blames it on mental illness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:26.200
then every criminal is going to use that excuse.&nbsp;
In fact, you already see that happening.

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:29.960
Seems&nbsp;like every apology video these days pulls&nbsp;the mental illness card. Now that’s not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:33.600
say that those people don’t deal with mental&nbsp;
health problems. And I’m not trying to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:37.040
that it isn’t a real issue. But think about&nbsp;
it like this. There are plenty of people with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:41.440
grief and depression who don’t try to kill their&nbsp;
dog and son. If you have mental issues that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:45.240
feel are controlling your behavior, it should&nbsp;
be your responsibility to get a hold of it.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:49.600
–[Amelia] If you touch my son again I’ll f*cking kill you!

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:53.552
But when it comes to puppet villains, 
it&nbsp;doesn’t always have to be a supernatural&nbsp;puppeteer.

00:14:53.552 --> 00:14:56.880
Take Stu Macher, from Scream.&nbsp;
He was roped into a murder scheme when his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:00.300
high school friend tried to get revenge on his&nbsp;
father’s adulterous mistress and her daughter.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:04.040
–[Sidney] What’s your motive?
–[Stu] Peer pressure. I'm far too sensitive.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:08.040
But none of the puppet villain answers&nbsp;were upvoted as much as this one.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:11.520
“Jaws. He was just living his&nbsp;best life doing what sharks do.”

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:15.401
True. True! Boy’s gotta eat.&nbsp;
That’s our next category.

00:15:15.401 --> 00:15:18.267
♪ Surf Music ♪

00:15:18.267 --> 00:15:22.551
We all know who Jaws is, right? 
Do I have to&nbsp;explain that one? He’s a shark that ate some&nbsp;people.

00:15:22.551 --> 00:15:26.229
This is actually the answer that&nbsp;inspired me to create different categories&nbsp;for this video.

00:15:26.229 --> 00:15:29.960
That, and the fact that I&nbsp;crave structure in my life, and in my videos.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:33.000
There are plenty of examples like&nbsp;this. Many of them are other animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:37.572
who are just naturally hunting to survive. 
I&nbsp;think someone said the Indoraptor from Jurassic&nbsp;World.

00:15:37.572 --> 00:15:44.160
Jurassic. Boys. Jurassic. Boys! I will&nbsp;
also add the Velociraptors from… Velocicoaster.

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:46.420
I know there’s gonna be some&nbsp;vegan out there who’s like…

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:48.680
– ACKCHYUALLY, allow me to correct you.

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:51.680
But these guys are CARNIVORES,&nbsp;they have to eat meat to survive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:54.960
Although the Vegan Velociraptor does&nbsp;sound like a funny comedy sketch.

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:59.423
It’s not just animals though. Several&nbsp;horror movie villains that I’ve talked&nbsp;about are deities. 

00:15:59.423 --> 00:16:03.555
Someone mentioned:&nbsp;“Sam from Trick 'r Treat...&nbsp;
He's just&nbsp;the enforcer of what man created.”&nbsp;

00:16:03.555 --> 00:16:05.320
Sam is basically a Halloween spirit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:08.700
who punishes those who do not adhere to the&nbsp;ancient traditions of the holiday, Samhain…

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:09.740
– Sam Hain.

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:13.960
… which was the precursor to our Halloween.&nbsp;
Speaking of people who deserved it,

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:19.680
this&nbsp;character named Emma tries to take the Halloween&nbsp;
decorations down ON THE NIGHT OF, then says this…

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:21.200
–[Emma] I hate Halloween.

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:24.674
Nobody could possibly be considered in the wrong&nbsp;for murdering this b***h.

00:16:24.674 --> 00:16:28.388
Similar&nbsp;to Sam (and also made by the same&nbsp;director) we have Krampus.

00:16:28.388 --> 00:16:30.600
Instead of&nbsp;enforcing traditions of Celtic folklore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:35.160
Krampus hails from Alpine and Nordic legends and&nbsp;
protects the ancient traditions around Christmas.

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:39.360
Someone on Threads mentioned The Killer Klowns&nbsp;
from Outer Space, arguing that we eat things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:44.400
less intelligent than we are, so why shouldn’t&nbsp;they? 
True! True, but on closer inspection I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:48.951
not sure if we should trust this guy because&nbsp;he appears to be a Killer Klown from Outer&nbsp;Space himself.

00:16:48.951 --> 00:16:52.120
He might be trying to get us&nbsp;to accept our fate as cotton candy treats.

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:56.120
Another one I thought of is Death from Final&nbsp;Destination. 
You could argue that Death is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.120 --> 00:17:00.240
not an entity, moreso a concept or force of&nbsp;nature, 
but Death is the closest thing we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:04.240
have to a villain in most of those movies. 
Each&nbsp;Final Destination film features a character who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:07.720
has a clairvoyant vision allowing the&nbsp;character to save his or herself and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:11.960
several others from a horrible disaster. 
But,&nbsp;because they were supposed to die that day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:15.660
Death keeps coming for them, and they&nbsp;
have to figure out how before it’s…

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:18.160
(Hit by Bus)
…too late.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:22.120
I have one more example, and while it’s&nbsp;
not the perfect case for virtuous villainy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.120 --> 00:17:25.693
it is unique enough to go in&nbsp;a category all on its own.

00:17:25.693 --> 00:17:28.680
♪ Spiritual Music ♪

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:33.440
Midsommar is the 2019 folk horror film directed&nbsp;by Ari Aster. 
At the beginning of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:38.040
the protagonist, Dani Ardor, loses her parents&nbsp;
and sister in a horrible tragedy, and six months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:41.760
later, her boyfriend invites her to go on a trip&nbsp;
to Sweden to experience the Midsommar festival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:46.380
at the home village of his friend, Pelle. 
Pelle&nbsp;is immediately interested in Dani coming with.

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:51.200
–[Pelle] You know, I’m very, very glad you’re&nbsp;
coming. I think it’s very good you’re coming.

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:55.320
Once there, he teaches Dani about how in their&nbsp;
culture, everything is experienced together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:58.840
including emotions like joy and sorrow.&nbsp;
The reason he was excited to have her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:01.640
on the trip is because she’s been&nbsp;grieving the loss of her family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:04.640
and *his* family can help her bear&nbsp;the weight of that emotional burden.

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:08.120
The American visitors are shocked and&nbsp;disgusted when they witness the Ättestupa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:13.320
a ritual suicide committed by the village elders&nbsp;after reaching age 72. But in their culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:15.240
this is normal, and it’s considered an honor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:19.080
because the elders know they will never grow old&nbsp;
enough to where caring for them becomes a burden.

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:22.800
In the end, each of the foreign visitors (other&nbsp;than Dani) break one of the cult's rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:26.160
and are offered up as part of a sacrifice&nbsp;at the end of the Midsummer celebration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:28.800
while Dani becomes their May&nbsp;Queen and joins the cult.

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:33.001
We learn that Pelle was planning it from the&nbsp;beginning. 
Their life cycle is broken up into&nbsp;seasons,

00:18:33.001 --> 00:18:36.960
and those aged 18-36 are sent on a&nbsp;pilgrimage to meet people outside the family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:40.040
and bring them back for the purposes&nbsp;of reproduction with genetic variation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:43.520
and, of course, to serve as fresh blood&nbsp;sacrifices during the burning ritual.

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:48.040
While the international guests were disturbed and&nbsp;
off-put seeing ritual suicide that they weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:53.488
used to, the cult members seem OK with it. 
There’s&nbsp;a whole other debate to be had about the ethics of&nbsp;euthanasia.

00:18:53.488 --> 00:18:57.077
It is mostly frowned upon throughout&nbsp;the world today, 
but there are major nations where&nbsp;it is legal,

00:18:57.077 --> 00:19:01.840
like Belgium, Luxembourg, Canada,&nbsp;New Zealand, Spain, the Netherlands and Colombia.

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:04.885
It’s a compelling debate that I’d be interested&nbsp;to learn more about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.885 --> 00:19:09.920
A lot of people online seem to support the idea, but obviously some are&nbsp;
against any form of taking human life.&nbsp;

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:14.080
It reminds me of the abortion debate, only it&nbsp;takes place on the other end of your life.

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:17.200
But even if we ignore this particular&nbsp;issue, I still can’t say that Pelle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:20.440
was in the right here. It’s one thing&nbsp;to sacrifice yourself if your culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:23.720
is accepting of it. He lost me when he&nbsp;sacrificed his friends, who didn’t know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:27.351
what they were getting themselves into. 
Pelle&nbsp;is definitely in the wrong on this one.&nbsp;

00:19:27.351 --> 00:19:31.640
Also,&nbsp;their family intentionally in-breeds one person&nbsp;
who they refer to as their “oracle”, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:35.640
they claim that the mental deformities give him&nbsp;
vision that is not clouded by normal cognition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:39.480
You’re intentionally giving someone a lifetime&nbsp;
of medical problems. That’s totally f*cked up.

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:43.600
But despite those issues, I think “They Were&nbsp;Only Bad To Us” is an interesting category,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:46.560
even though I could only think of&nbsp;one example, and that one example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:50.200
doesn’t exactly qualify… If you can think&nbsp;of any others, let me know in the comments.

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:53.680
I think the takeaway here is that&nbsp;most villains aren’t perfect angels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:57.040
and if they were, they wouldn’t really be&nbsp;villains would they? But the best ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.040 --> 00:20:01.339
the ones that are the most fun to talk about,&nbsp;
are the ones that have people on both sides&nbsp;of the aisle. 

00:20:01.339 --> 00:20:04.720
The ones where you *can* have a&nbsp;discussion that turns into a discussion of ethics.

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:08.720
A lot of YouTubers like to say, “OK, let’s&nbsp;
keep it civil in the comments.” But honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:11.600
I kinda don’t care. Feel free to rip&nbsp;each other apart in the comments.

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:16.742
But in my opinion, it isn’t that relevant&nbsp;if the person is actually in the right or&nbsp;in the wrong.

00:20:16.742 --> 00:20:19.800
It is those who *think* they&nbsp;are in the right, or at the very least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:23.160
*understand* why they are in the wrong and&nbsp;still see it fit to just push on anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:26.040
who make the most compelling&nbsp;and memorable villains.

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:29.480
OK, I was about to finish this video, but&nbsp;
we’ve got a late submission in the 11th hour.

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:31.720
“The Blind Guy from Don’t Breathe.”

00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:35.560
Girl. Girl! Do you not remember&nbsp;the second half of that movie?

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:40.491
But if you want to understand these morally&nbsp;
ambiguous malefactors on a deeper level, you’re in&nbsp;luck!

00:20:40.491 --> 00:20:45.867
Because I’ve already done deep dive analyses&nbsp;
on many of them in my amazing and wonderful show called Horror History. &nbsp;&nbsp;

